From 59f734320b72d6d31762786cb90dae84fbc2c4ec Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Giuseppe Maggio <jertlok@proton.me>
Date: Sat, 19 Sep 2026 18:35:21 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] wifi: ath10k: trust the WMI service map for WoWLAN on SNOC

Every SNOC target has WoWLAN disabled no matter how capable it is, because the
gate is a feature bit in a file that cannot carry one.

ath10k_wow_init() returns early unless ATH10K_FW_FEATURE_WOWLAN_SUPPORT is set
in the firmware file. On SNOC the firmware is not in that file: it is loaded by
the WLAN remoteproc and handed over by QMI, and firmware-5.bin is metadata
only. On WCN3990 hw1.0 it is 60 bytes, and its IEs are FW_VERSION, TIMESTAMP,
OTP_IMAGE and WMI_OP_VERSION -- there is no FEATURES IE at all, so no feature
bit is ever set and the early return is unconditional.

The WMI service map is the firmware describing itself, so on this bus it is the
authoritative answer rather than the cross-check it can be elsewhere. Use it in
place of the file's bit for SNOC and leave the WARN_ON for every other bus,
where the file does describe the firmware that will run.

Measured on a Pixel 2 XL (WCN3990, msm8998), with CONFIG_ATH10K_DEBUGFS:

    # grep -i wow /sys/kernel/debug/ieee80211/phy0/ath10k/wmi_services
    WMI_SERVICE_WOW       enabled
    WMI_SERVICE_D0WOW     enabled

while `iw phy0 info` showed no WoWLAN section at all, because wiphy->wowlan was
never assigned.

The cost of leaving it as it is falls on suspend rather than on WoWLAN users.
cfg80211's wiphy_suspend() takes the wowlan path only if wiphy.wowlan_config is
set, which requires wiphy->wowlan to exist; otherwise it falls through to
cfg80211_leave_all() and tears down every interface. So on these devices every
system suspend disconnects the WLAN outright, and resume rebuilds the stack and
re-associates from scratch. Measured with pm_print_times on the same device,
wiphy_resume costs 313 ms of a 576 ms device-resume budget, and the association
does not complete until 2.6 s after PM: suspend exit.

Assisted-by: LLM
Signed-off-by: Giuseppe Maggio <jertlok@proton.me>
---
 drivers/net/wireless/ath/ath10k/wow.c | 22 ++++++++++++++++++++--
 1 file changed, 20 insertions(+), 2 deletions(-)

diff --git a/drivers/net/wireless/ath/ath10k/wow.c b/drivers/net/wireless/ath/ath10k/wow.c
index 92af8bc63362..df4e23aabea4 100644
--- a/drivers/net/wireless/ath/ath10k/wow.c
+++ b/drivers/net/wireless/ath/ath10k/wow.c
@@ -614,8 +614,26 @@ int ath10k_wow_op_resume(struct ieee80211_hw *hw)
 
 int ath10k_wow_init(struct ath10k *ar)
 {
-	if (!test_bit(ATH10K_FW_FEATURE_WOWLAN_SUPPORT,
-		      ar->running_fw->fw_file.fw_features))
+	bool fw_supports_wow = test_bit(ATH10K_FW_FEATURE_WOWLAN_SUPPORT,
+					ar->running_fw->fw_file.fw_features);
+
+	/*
+	 * On SNOC the firmware is not in the firmware file. It is loaded by the
+	 * WLAN remoteproc and handed over by QMI, and firmware-5.bin is
+	 * metadata only -- for WCN3990 hw1.0 it is 60 bytes carrying FW_VERSION,
+	 * TIMESTAMP, OTP_IMAGE and WMI_OP_VERSION, with no FEATURES IE at all.
+	 * Its feature bits therefore say nothing about the firmware that is
+	 * actually running, and every SNOC target ends up with WoWLAN disabled
+	 * however capable it is.
+	 *
+	 * The WMI service map is the firmware describing itself, so on this bus
+	 * it is the authoritative answer rather than a cross-check. WCN3990 on a
+	 * Pixel 2 XL reports WMI_SERVICE_WOW and WMI_SERVICE_D0WOW enabled.
+	 */
+	if (ar->hif.bus == ATH10K_BUS_SNOC)
+		fw_supports_wow = test_bit(WMI_SERVICE_WOW, ar->wmi.svc_map);
+
+	if (!fw_supports_wow)
 		return 0;
 
 	if (WARN_ON(!test_bit(WMI_SERVICE_WOW, ar->wmi.svc_map)))
-- 
2.55.0

